\f0\fs24 \cf0 Elders of Israel came to Ezekiel and sat before him, presumably to consult the LORD through him.  But they combined this supposed consultation with having set up idols in their own hearts.  This is like a man who wishes to run to safety, but then stops to install a large cinder block right in front of him that he promptly trips on when he runs, smashing his face into the ground.  This is, I think, how we ought to see claiming we have contact with God, whether through church or prayer or Bible reading, when we also set up idols in our own hearts.  The LORD says something similar - he calls it setting up an iniquitous stumbling block in front of one\'92s own face.  \
\
In response to that rather self defeating behavior, the LORD asks, should I let myself be consulted by them?  There is some irony here.  The LORD\'92s prophet stands in his counsel, and hears the LORD\'92s thoughts.  But the elders misunderstand the nature of the LORD\'92s prophet.  They think the prophet can get knowledge, or even exercise some measure of control over the LORD.  Pagan prophets operated that way - Baalam, famously, even tried something fairly exactly like that with the LORD once.  And these people, having idols in their heart, want to treat the LORD like they would their other gods.  Lest we be like the man praying \'91God, I thank you that I am not like other men\'92, we ought to examine ourselves - it is all too easy to slip into assuming that despite being the creator of heaven and earth, the one true God conforms to human ideas about what a god should be.\
\
And, of course, though the message was to the house of Israel, the command to repent and turn away from your idols should be taken seriously by everyone.  People who can honestly say they have never bowed down to a statue or an image can still have idols in their hearts.  That is to say, created things, given the kind of worship which ought to be reserved for the creator, can be our focus.  In the Hebrew Bible, the heart is not only the seat of emotion, but of thought, intention, and will.  Moreover, Ezekiel sharpens both his diagnosis and his polemic with the metaphor of a stumbling block of iniquity before a person\'92s face.  What we turn our face towards is a matter of commitment - consider someone putting a picture of a loved one on a physical desktop or a computer desktop or a phone login screen.  What we turn our face towards is a matter of attention, what we have chosen to put before our face.  But sadly, that choice can be to put attention towards a stumbling block of iniquity.\
\
And so, the LORD asks Ezekiel in verse 3, \'93Should I indeed let myself be consulted by them?\'94  They are attempting to consult the God who rightly allows no rivals while holding rivals in their hearts.  They and their whole people have, as chapter 8 puts it, put the branch to the nose, filling the nation with violence and abominations, provoking the LORD to wrath, so that he will not hear them even if they cry in his ears with a loud voice.  That gives a hint that the LORD will do more than simply not responding.  Verse 4, the LORD will respond to such a person according to the multitude of his idols.  And verse 5 tells us the purpose for such a response.  The context for verse 5 is the justice of responding in wrath of verse 4.  But the announced purpose is to seize the hearts of the people of Israel.  That seizure is capture, as in war or by force.  Perhaps the people are under the misperception that they have some sort of hold on the LORD such that they can make him give the answer they want.  But it is the LORD who will lay hold of their very thoughts, because their thoughts are the problem, the battleground, and the goal.  The people have estranged themselves.  But the character of the LORD is such that while he is just, and holy, he is also full of mercy and lovingkindness.  And so, he will not leave them estranged, though they deserve it.  He will seize them to bring them back to himself, or as the end of this oracle restates the LORD\'92s purpose, \'91that they may be my people and I may be their God\'92.  The idols in their hearts and the resulting estrangement are a state of affairs that the LORD has chosen to act against - rather different from the implied response the elders were seeking through Ezekiel. \
\
But Ezekiel is not just commanded to speak this way to reason with the people about why the LORD will respond in this way.  In a very real way, he is laying down legislation, enforced by the LORD.  Or, perhaps more accurately, he is interpreting existing legislation, specifically Leviticus 17, which regulated sacrifice so as to end sacrificing sacrificing to goat demons.  There are several exact verbal links, some of them repeated, to Leviticus 17.  Some of these are found many other places in Torah, especially Leviticus, but that does not lessen the sense one gets that part of the response the LORD gives is something along the lines of \'91I already gave you the Torah to forbid what you are doing and warn you that I will cut you off from your people if you do it\'92.  \
	But the message Ezekiel is given goes beyond interpretation, making explicit that the Torah very much does still apply even to matters of idols in the heart.  Ezekiel is to announce the LORD\'92s policy of answering not their question, but their thoughts.  Those less familiar with Ezekiel and more familiar with Jesus may find it easier to understand this by remembering how Jesus constantly answered not what people spoke aloud, but the sins they thought concealed in their thoughts.  The idols, even though they are secretly kept in the heart, will get a response in wrath.  Though the LORD warns them to repent, as in verse 6, lest they separate themselves from the LORD, as in verse 7, earlier chapters of Ezekiel imply that the nation as a whole is rebellious and will not do so.  And so, the punishment in this legislation is not a hypothetical.  The LORD will make such a person a sign and a byword and cut him off from the midst of his people, as in verse 8.  The man with idols in his heart will be cut off in such a way that he will still be a sign to the nation and to other nations, just an involuntary one.  The nature of the remnant is at stake - the LORD says in verse 8 that he is doing this so that they will know he is the LORD, or, later, in verse 11, to end the straying and defiling themselves and not being the LORD\'92s people, as he has been hinting at with the interjections of I, the LORD.\
	For this is what is at stake, for the original hearers, and for us.  Are our hearts directed towards the LORD, is he our God in truth, do we love the LORD our God with all our heart, all our soul, all our might?  What direction is our face pointed?  What direction is God\'92s face pointed - does his face shine on us, or has he set his face against us, as in verse 8?\
	Verse 9 seems to swerve into new territory, bringing up the possibility that someone who claims to speak for God will speak falsely - but verse 10, in turn, merges this with the ongoing discussion of punishment for those who inquire of the LORD in the wrong way.  The possibility of false prophets, of course, brings up the awful danger of being misled on a matter so crucial.  But the way the LORD brings up the matter does not soften the matter by attributing this only to a lying human, though chapter 13 has just discussed such.  No, the LORD says it bluntly, \'91I, the LORD, have deceived that prophet\'92.  Deuteronomy 13 warned that there would be false prophets, saying \'91the LORD your God is testing you to know whether you love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul\'92.  This is the problem - they do not, in fact, love the LORD in this way.  They want to be deceived.  Consider the example of Ahab.  Ahab had both false prophets and a true one.  But of the true prophet, Micaiah, he said \'91I hate him, for he never prophesies good concerning me\'92.  The true prophet was even told by messenger to conform to the liars all around him and say something the king wanted to hear.  So the king very much wanted to be deceived.  And yet, it was the LORD who deceived him.  The LORD asked various spirits who would entice Ahab, that Ahab might believe falsely and thus take actions leading to his death.  A spirit, with not only permission but command, went out to be a lying spirit in the mouth of Ahab\'92s prophets.  So, it is true that the LORD gave Ahab the deception he very clearly wanted.  It is true that the LORD warned Ahab of the fact, the method, and the reason for the deception.  Nonetheless the LORD was the one who put a lying spirit in the mouth of all these prophets.  The text is quite clear - though in a way, a foreign army executed the LORD\'92s judgement on Ahab, the LORD brought Ahab to the point that army would kill him by deceiving him in exactly the way he asked to be deceived.  Ahab\'92s reaction to both true and false prophet reveals him as not just deceivable, but going so far as to misuse his kingly authority to demand to be deceived.  \
\
In a similar way, the nation got, from the hand of the LORD, the false prophets it deserved.  They did not love the LORD, but rather had idols on their hearts, so they failed the test.  They wanted to be deceived, therefore part of the appropriate punishment was deceiving them - even as in Exodus, Pharaoh hardened his heart, and so the LORD hardened his heart, and in so doing accomplished his purposes clearly laid out in advance.  And as with the false prophet who opposed Micaiah in front of Ahab, as with Pharaoh, the false prophets get the punishment they deserve. \
\
Both inquiring with idols in the heart and deceiving as a false prophet have a certain similarity to them.  One says words out loud according to social reality, as if God does not hear what one says in the silence of the heart.  And so the punishment on both is the same, verse 10, \'92the punishment of the prophet and the punishment of the inquirer shall be alike\'92  Same punishment, same purpose: to end the straying.  \
	To generalize the message here, God demands practical monotheism, not just saying that there is one God, but acting like it, thinking like it.  God demands that people come to him on his own terms only.  Even if an entire nation\'92s religious community says that these are the terms on which one approaches God, those might be deceivers, a test for the nation whether they truly love the LORD with all their heart.  Only the terms God himself gives are acceptable.  Those who assist others in approaching God the wrong way are not safe.  Those who want to be deceived are not safe.  Those who go along with what everyone else says is acceptable are not safe.  This oracle offers no safety, except in obedience to the command from Deuteronomy 10:12-13, \'91And now, Israel, what does the Lord your God require of you, but to fear the Lord your God, to walk in all his ways, to love him, to serve the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul, and to keep the commandments and statutes of the Lord, which I am commanding you today for your good?\'92.  Neither does the next oracle, which starts in verse 12.\
\
\
Verse 12 is both a marker that this is a new oracle, and a reminder of the LORD from whom that oracle comes.  Similarly, starting the oracle \'92son of man\'92 is a reminder both that a human has been brought into the counsel of his creator, and the gap between humanity and creator.\
\
The punishments of this oracle are, grammatically at least, hypothetical.  Not for the first time in Ezekiel, they are drawn from Leviticus 26.  Even the land to be punished is not specified - though the crime of treachery against the LORD is of course a rather pointed hypothetical.  Both the hypothetical and the standard nature of the punishments allow the oracle to focus on the second half of the if:  if even the most righteous of men were in a land, somehow brought there from different eras, they would not deliver that land.  Not from punishment for treachery.  \
\
In chapter 13, the false prophets of Israel were faulted for not going up into the breaches or building a wall, to protect the people against the day of the LORD.  But here, not even well known examples of great righteousness would be enough to build a wall that would protect the people.  Not against this day of the LORD.  Not this people, given their rebellion.  \
\
Not Noah, the righteous and blameless amidst a world of violence, who saved his children though the world was whelmed in flood.  Here, he could not save even his children.\
\
Not Daniel, exile of the wave of exiles Ezekiel was sent to, known for his loyalty to the LORD and his high position.  The exiles may have hoped there was a second Daniel who would somehow save Jerusalem.  But even if there were - he could save only himself.  He could not save a hypothetical treacherous land.  As verse 21 says, how much less Jerusalem.\
\
Not Job, upon whose entire narrative turns his blamelessness, who affirms his righteousness.  He could pray for his friends and they would be forgiven.  But here, like Abraham interceded for Sodom but Sodom was still destroyed, or Ezekiel was not even permitted to intercede for his people, no one would be able to effectively intercede for such a land.\
\
No, not even these three could deliver so much as a single person, excepting only themselves - they, the righteous would be delivered, but they would be the only righteous.  Not when the LORD stretches his hand out against that land, as in verse 13.  As the LORD stretched out his hand in the past to deliver, with the manyfold disasters striking their enemies, so he would stretch out his hand against his own people, with none to deliver.  The LORD alone was the deliverer they needed, and they had acted faithlessly towards him.\
\
Because the people fit the hypothetical of verses 12-20, the reasonable law laid down in that hypothetical seals their doom.  And their own case is worse than the hypothetical case, and so, as verse 21 says, \'91how much more\'92 their doom cannot be averted.  Perhaps it was easier to accept the hypothetical case than the case they were so attached to.  Perhaps an example made it easier to comprehend for the original audience.  But then, while we don\'92t have an exact match to their example, we have reason to believe that the fate of Jerusalem and the principles behind it are an example to us.  That should be sobering.\
\
But perhaps more sobering should be the sudden turn this oracle takes in verse 22.  The entire oracle has repeatedly warned that even the great righteous men would not be able to deliver even their own children from the coming judgement.  So, surprise - there will be survivors.  Some sons and daughters will be brought out.  \
\
But how?  It cannot be by the righteousness of some great man, that has just been conclusively denied.  By their own righteousness?  No.  The LORD must have done this, for his own reasons.  The consolation the exiles experience upon seeing the survivors will not be seeing that some were rescued out of the disaster, or seeing that there were righteous indeed in Jerusalem - there were not any righteous. That consolation will be from seeing the ways and deeds of the survivors.  But those ways and deeds will bring consolation in the face of the LORD having acted in wrath against his people.  The survivors will cling to their ways even after having seen first hand the justice of the LORD.  And so, as the chapter ends, the exiles will know that even in the extreme case of all he did against Jerusalem, the LORD has not acted without cause.  This is the promise the LORD makes in this oracle - you will know.  \
\
It is the very unrighteousness of the survivors that casts the righteousness and justice of the LORD into view.  However extreme the four fold punishment, famine, beasts, sword, and pestilence, might seem, the example of Noah, Daniel, and Job tells us that any intuitions we might have about righteous or innocent people living in the land rescuing the land are incorrect in the face of the kind of treachery Jerusalem engaged in.  No, the LORD would be upright and just to stretch out his hand against such treachery, against people like the exiles could see that the survivors were.  That uprightness and justice, seen more clearly, would allow the exiles to see that the LORD is by no means arbitrary - or so I generalize \'91not without cause\'92.  And, because the LORD is not at all arbitrary, he may be counted on and trusted, which is the true antidote to the treachery and rebellion which had poisoned the entire people, the exiles included.  It is precisely that trust which will give comfort when they see the unrighteousness of the remnant.\
\
Ezekiel, of course, seeks to guide their understanding to that point by speaking this way, even though they will not understand until they see God\'92s wrath and the reasons for it in the person of the remnant from Jerusalem.  That is, while Ezekiel predicts what will happen, this is to bring the people to the right interpretation of what will happen when it happens, and to repentance as a result.  Moreover, Ezekiel has also been reminding his audience of other reasons for believing the LORD not arbitrary in his wrath, reasons brought up in earlier oracles.  The LORD warned of these very punishments in Leviticus 26.  That is, in punishing this way, the LORD is keeping a covenant which was known for centuries.  The same covenant the people are breaking is the thing the LORD is keeping by punishing to this extreme.\
\
Similarly, this is not the first time that Ezekiel has brought up the LORD\'92s way acting in proportion to the evil of his people\'92s ways.  Chapter 7 especially emphasized this, repeating that the LORD would judge his people according to their ways, ending with \'93According to their way I will do to them, and according to their judgments I will judge them, and they shall know that I am the LORD\'94.  It is their practices, their deeds, their standards, their justice system, which provide the measure of the wrath the LORD will bring.  The furthest thing from arbitrary.  The ways of the people judged will in turn reveal that the LORD\'92s ways are, among other things, perfect justice.\
\
The character and ways of the LORD are always, for everyone, worth knowing.  But some aspects of this oracle can seem distant to us.  So I close with an observation, and two questions.\
\
First, an observation.  Much of the oracle speaks of hypothetical cases in which even the most righteous men could deliver neither their sons nor their daughters.  How much less can we save our children.  Our children will not enter the kingdom of God because we are righteous.  We have, of ourselves, no way of making sure our children find salvation.  We ought to guide our children to keep the way of the LORD by doing righteousness and justice.  Without keeping the way of the LORD themselves, our children cannot be saved, but we cannot make our children be saved or guarantee our children are saved.  But notice the hope in the passive of verse 22.  Some survivors will be left in it, sons and daughters who will be brought out.  While those sons and daughters were rescued to display the righteousness of God through their own unrighteousness, it was the LORD who rescued them.  It is in the LORD rescuing that we must place our hope, for our children, and indeed for anyone else.  And, unlike Ezekiel, we are not forbidden to intercede.   1 John 5:16 places some limitations on our intercession, but overall Scripture strongly encourages us to intercessory prayer.\
\
Second, a question.  To what degree have today\'92s nations and churches acted faithlessly towards the LORD?  There are extreme differences between the current situation and the one Ezekiel wrote into, and we must take account of those differences when answering that question.  But after accounting for that, this is a question we must face honestly.\
\
Third, a final question.  To what degree are our ways such that, should they be revealed fully, the LORD\'92s justice in punishing us would also thereby be revealed?\
\
\
\
}